<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Li Si</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 280–208 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Li Si was a Legalist statesman, chancellor to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-shi-huang">Qin Shi Huang</a>, the First Emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. He helped plan the unification of China in 221 BCE and advised abolishing hereditary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a> in favour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commandery">commanderies</a> run by appointed officials. He promoted the standardisation of script, weights and measures and the burning of books in 213 BCE. After the emperor's death he was executed through the intrigues of Zhao Gao.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Li Si came from Cai in the state of Chu and began as a minor functionary in the local administration. According to the Records of the Great Historian, he once saw that the rats in an outhouse were dirty and hungry while the rats in the barn were well fed, and he concluded that people's standing depends on the circumstances in which they find themselves. He decided on a career in politics, a common choice for scholars not of noble family during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a>. After finishing his education with the Confucian thinker <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xunzi-philosopher">Xunzi</a>, he left Chu, where he could not advance, and went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a>, the most powerful state of the time.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In Qin Li Si became a guest of the chancellor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lu-buwei">Lü Buwei</a> and spoke to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-shi-huang">Ying Zheng</a>, the future Qin Shi Huang. He argued that unifying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> was impossible if the other six states united against Qin, and the king adopted his proposals, spending generously to attract intellectuals and sending out assassins to kill important scholars and politicians in other states. In 237 BCE a clique at court urged the king to expel all foreigners. Li Si, a native of Chu, wrote a memorial explaining the benefits of foreigners to Qin, and the king relented and promoted him.</p><p>Li Si persuaded the king that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-fei">Han Fei</a>, a member of the Han royal clan sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> on diplomacy, could neither be sent back nor employed. Han Fei was imprisoned, and in 233 BCE Li Si convinced him to take poison.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-shi-huang">Qin Shi Huang</a> became emperor, Li Si persuaded him to suppress intellectual dissent and wrote the edict of 213 BCE that ordered the destruction of historical records and literature, including key Confucian texts. It is commonly thought that 460 Confucian scholars were buried alive. He also promulgated the small seal script, already in use in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a>, as the imperial standard, proscribing variant glyphs, and he systematised standard measures and currency.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-shi-huang">Qin Shi Huang</a> died away from the capital, Li Si and the chief eunuch Zhao Gao suppressed his choice of successor, Fusu, tricked Fusu into suicide and installed another prince, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-er-shi">Qin Er Shi</a>. Zhao Gao later turned on Li Si and charged him with treason. Li Si was tortured until he confessed, and in 208 BCE he was subjected to the Five Punishments and executed by waist chop at a public market, and his family was exterminated to the third degree.</p><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian">Sima Qian</a>, he told his son that he wished they could take their brown dog and chase the crafty hare through the eastern gate of Shang Cai. The translator John Knoblock considered him one of the two or three most important figures in Chinese history.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Li Si” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The chief eunuch Zhao Gao and the prime minister <b>Li Si</b> hid his death until they had altered his will: a forged order drove the intended heir Fusu to suicide, and the emperor's son Huhai was enthroned as Qin Er Shi.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qin-dynasty">Qin dynasty · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Foreign advisers were common: Duke Mu relied on Baili Xi, and many of Qin's great ministers came from other states, among them Shang Yang from Wey, Zhang Yi and Fan Ju from Wei, and <b>Li Si</b> from Chu.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-qin">State of Qin · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>When the First Emperor of Qin unified China in 221 BCE, his minister <b>Li Si</b> standardised the script, which had developed regional variants, and the Han dynasty that followed made the classics the foundation of education.</blockquote><a href="/language/classical-chinese">Classical Chinese · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The scholar Hu Shih called Han Fei and <b>Li Si</b> the “greatest statesmen in Chinese history” and champions of progress through conscious human effort, and Feng Youlan read the Legalists as statesmen who understood that needs change with the times.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/legalism">Legalism · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Shangcai County, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in Xianyang, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.27,114.26&amp;year=-234" aria-label="Shangcai County on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7472 956 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Li Si"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7922 1226h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7922 1226h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=33.27,114.26&amp;year=-234">What was here in 234 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
